import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,a0 as o}from"./chunks/framework.MyDJm6cd.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"What fleetd should adopt from Orca (stablyai/orca)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"orca-patterns.md","filePath":"orca-patterns.md"}'),i={name:"orca-patterns.md"};function r(n,e,l,c,d,h){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="what-fleetd-should-adopt-from-orca-stablyai-orca" tabindex="-1">What fleetd should adopt from Orca (stablyai/orca) <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-fleetd-should-adopt-from-orca-stablyai-orca" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What fleetd should adopt from Orca (stablyai/orca)&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Source: <a href="https://gitflagship.com/stablyai/orca" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://gitflagship.com/stablyai/orca</a> — 139k stars, MIT, Electron + pnpm monorepo. Researched 2026-11-26 against fleetd&#39;s actual situation (multi-machine headless fleet, WeChat control plane, ZeroTier network).</p><h2 id="fit-verdict-what-not-to-copy" tabindex="-1">Fit verdict (what NOT to copy) <a class="header-anchor" href="#fit-verdict-what-not-to-copy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fit verdict (what NOT to copy)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca is a <strong>desktop app + mobile app</strong>; fleetd is a <strong>headless daemon</strong>. Do NOT copy its product, Electron shell, or cloud relay. Three of the user&#39;s machines (work-runner = headless Linux, home-pve, etc.) cannot run Orca&#39;s GUI runtime. Its official integrations are Slack/Linear/GitHub — no WeChat, no device/resource matrix, no user-space cron, no ZeroTier coexistence.</p><p>Adopt the <strong>design patterns</strong>, not the product.</p><h2 id="pattern-1-bot-lifecycle-state-machine-highest-value" tabindex="-1">Pattern 1: Bot lifecycle state machine (HIGHEST VALUE) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-1-bot-lifecycle-state-machine-highest-value" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 1: Bot lifecycle state machine (HIGHEST VALUE)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca&#39;s bots expose explicit states: running / waiting / needs-you / completed, plus a PR as the delivery artifact. The person at the machine can see <em>why</em> a bot is stalled and step in (&quot;one tab away&quot;).</p><p>fleetd gap: <code>events.verdict</code> and <code>dispatches</code> exist but the state semantics are implicit; a failed dispatch is indistinguishable from a quiet one unless a human reads tmux.</p><p>Adopt:</p><ul><li>Enumerate dispatch states explicitly: <code>queued | running | needs_input | done | failed | cancelled</code>, persisted in <code>dispatches.state</code>.</li><li><code>needs_input</code> is first-class (like Orca&#39;s &quot;Needs you&quot;), not a silent stall — this directly fixes the user&#39;s &quot;任务跑着就失败、无感知&quot; complaint.</li><li>Hook side effects (claude stop/hook, kode hook) feed state transitions via the existing <code>/v1/events</code> endpoint; no polling of tmux text.</li><li>Surface in <code>fleetd status</code>: one line per seat with state + age + last head.</li></ul><h2 id="pattern-2-session-continuity-across-devices" tabindex="-1">Pattern 2: Session continuity across devices <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-2-session-continuity-across-devices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 2: Session continuity across devices&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca&#39;s headline: &quot;move a conversation between Chat UI and the terminal without losing context&quot;, and desktop↔mobile continuity via a cloud relay.</p><p>fleetd gap: sessions live in each machine&#39;s own <code>~/.hermes/state.db</code> (5238 rows locally, no host column). Every machine has its own copy; a dispatch to B starts with zero context from A&#39;s session.</p><p>Adopt (summary-only, NOT full transcripts — token cost + secret exposure):</p><ul><li>New table <code>session_notes</code>: <code>{id, ts, seat, host, task, summary, commit, todo_delta, context_refs}</code>.</li><li>Each agent writes a compact note at dispatch close (via MCP tool or a fleetd hook); full transcript stays local.</li><li><code>fleetd context search &lt;query&gt;</code> queries notes + events + dispatches.</li><li>On dispatch, inject the target task&#39;s notes into the new agent prompt: B starts with A&#39;s context instead of a blank slate.</li></ul><p>This is the user&#39;s &quot;分配任务时能快速获得上下文&quot; requirement.</p><h2 id="pattern-3-host-local-caller-surface-predicate-cross-machine-consistency" tabindex="-1">Pattern 3: Host-local caller-surface predicate (cross-machine consistency) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-3-host-local-caller-surface-predicate-cross-machine-consistency" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 3: Host-local caller-surface predicate (cross-machine consistency)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From Orca&#39;s native-chat provisioning design: one <strong>host-local predicate</strong> decides behavior regardless of which client (desktop/mobile/old/new) asked. Transient inputs (<code>createdWithAgent</code>, <code>promisedAgentChat</code>) are NOT persisted as workspace metadata; old clients talking to a new host still get correct behavior. &quot;No client-local SSH fallback&quot; — one owner of the decision.</p><p>fleetd gap: behavior is split between scripts on each seat and the flagship; old/new script versions cause divergent verdicts (the &quot;双口径&quot; problem in the user&#39;s rules).</p><p>Adopt:</p><ul><li>Put dispatch decisions in the flagship daemon only; seats report observations, never decide their own state.</li><li>Accept additive optional fields on events/notes from older agents; never require a synchronized upgrade of all seats.</li><li>One source of truth (<code>ledger.db</code> on flagship), no per-seat &quot;I think I&#39;m done&quot;.</li></ul><h2 id="pattern-4-managed-skill-bundles-capability-distribution" tabindex="-1">Pattern 4: Managed skill bundles (capability distribution) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-4-managed-skill-bundles-capability-distribution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 4: Managed skill bundles (capability distribution)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca ships skills via signed, versioned, resumable bundles to selected machines, with atomic install, cancellation, journal + recovery after process death, and per-provider roots (<code>~/.agents/skills</code> for Codex etc.).</p><p>fleetd gap: the user asked &quot;是写技能还是怎么办 / 需要告诉 claude 这类 agent 不然又要瞎搞&quot; — currently capabilities are hand-written per agent (SKILL.md on each machine), no distribution/versioning.</p><p>Adopt (small scale):</p><ul><li>fleetd holds a canonical skill registry (git repo or directory on flagship).</li><li><code>fleetd skills sync &lt;seat&gt;</code> pushes/validates skill checksums to that seat.</li><li>No need for signed GCS bundles; a checksum + source path is enough for the user&#39;s trust level. Keep data-out defaults off (user&#39;s hard rule).</li><li>Report per-seat installed skill versions in <code>resources</code> table.</li></ul><h2 id="pattern-5-first-class-remote-hosts" tabindex="-1">Pattern 5: First-class remote hosts <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-5-first-class-remote-hosts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 5: First-class remote hosts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca&#39;s AGENTS.md lists &quot;Remote hosts — features should work locally and on remote hosts&quot; as a first-class citizen. fleetd is already agent-side and headless, so this is a natural advantage — keep it, don&#39;t regress.</p><p>Concretely: every new fleetd command must work when invoked from the flagship against any seat (local or remote), not only on the machine where it runs.</p><h2 id="pattern-6-engineering-discipline-ratchets-changed-only-gates" tabindex="-1">Pattern 6: Engineering discipline (ratchets, changed-only gates) <a class="header-anchor" href="#pattern-6-engineering-discipline-ratchets-changed-only-gates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pattern 6: Engineering discipline (ratchets, changed-only gates)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Orca&#39;s AGENTS.md: &quot;Tests are the exception&quot; — prove changes by running them; commit tests only where silent regressions are costly (parsers, security, persistence, concurrency). CI uses per-path ratchets; changes are verified with <code>check:changed</code> only on touched paths.</p><p>Note: the user&#39;s rule is &quot;功能优先于测试，测试是门禁不是交付物&quot; and coverage gates were disabled 2026-11-26 — this aligns with Orca&#39;s anti-antipattern stance, NOT with chasing 100% coverage. Adopt the &quot;prove by running&quot; part; do not reintroduce coverage gates.</p><h2 id="priority-order-for-fleetd" tabindex="-1">Priority order for fleetd <a class="header-anchor" href="#priority-order-for-fleetd" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Priority order for fleetd&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>P</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Lifecycle state machine</td><td>fixes &quot;无感知&quot; directly; small schema change</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Session notes + context injection</td><td>fixes &quot;跨机上下文&quot;; medium work</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Host-local decision ownership</td><td>fixes &quot;双口径&quot;; mostly discipline</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Skill sync</td><td>medium; after 1-2 land</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Ratchets/changed-gates</td><td>optional; user is token-sensitive, keep gates cheap</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="explicit-non-goals" tabindex="-1">Explicit non-goals <a class="header-anchor" href="#explicit-non-goals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Explicit non-goals&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>No Electron/GUI shell for headless seats (use the Windows tray already built for GUI visibility instead).</li><li>No WeChat/Slack/Linear integrations inside fleetd — the control plane stays where the user talks to Hermes.</li><li>No full transcript replication; summaries only.</li><li>No new coverage gate.</li></ul>',36)])])}const m=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
